# The release+tag process for Jove on github (20260823)

[Assumes this is all run on an x86_64 machine, adapt if
you're on something else]

When tagging a new release (e.g. 5.0.0) on github after
suitable testing of the candidate, ensure the Changelog is
updated and commit.

From
https://github.com/jonmacs/jove/actions/workflows/testbuild.yml
choose the latest build, which ought to be all green.

Download the following output files (the number ? after
container or hosted might differ slightly depending on the
order they run in)

In a temporary directory, download:

DIST-container-debian_11_amd64.zip
DIST-container-alpine_latest_amd64.zip
DIST-container-alpine_latest_386.zip
DIST-container-centos_7_amd64.zip
DIST-hosted-ubuntu-24.04-arm.zip

unzip -x -j DIST-container-debian_11_amd64.zip '*/jove_*.deb' '*/*.tgz' '*/*.zip'
unzip -x -j DIST-container-centos_7_amd64.zip '*/*.rpm'
unzip -x -j DIST-container-alpine_latest_amd64.zip '*/*static.tar.gz'
unzip -x -j DIST-container-alpine_latest_386.zip '*/*static.tar.gz'
unzip -x -j DIST-hosted-ubuntu-24.04-arm.zip '*/jove_*.deb'

rm DIST-*.zip

Something that looks like the following files should remain (5.0.0
will be whatever Jove version you are releasing)

jove-5.0.0-10.el7.x86_64.rpm
jove_5.0.0-1_amd64.deb
jove_5.0.0-1_arm64.deb
jove_5.0.0-1_i386.deb
jove-5.0.0-i586-alpine-linux-musl-static.tar.gz
jove-5.0.0-i686-w64-mingw32.zip
jove500s.zip
jove-5.0.0.tgz
jove-5.0.0-x86_64-alpine-linux-musl-static.tar.gz
jove-5.0.0-x86_64-w64-mingw32.zip

I run pkg/dos/testdos.sh to generate a MS-DOS
DIST/joveNNNd.zip (copy over to the temporary directory)

Generate a self-extracting form using the following command (all lines in one go)
(replacing 500 with the current abbreviated version)

	curl -L https://raw.githubusercontent.com/jonmacs/jove/binary-unzipsfx/pkg/dos/UNZIPSFX.EXE -o DIST/unzipsfx.exe &&
	sha256sum -c pkg/dos/unzipsfx.sha256 && # should say DIST/unzipsfx.exe: OK
	cat DIST/unzipsfx.exe DIST/jove500d.zip > DIST/jove500d.exe &&
	zip -A DIST/jove500d.exe

After the following testing, upload those as assets to the
tag.  Filter the most relevant user-facing or
packager-affecting changes from ChangeLog.

To test, run a container (podman) of an ubuntu with this dir
exported to it with -v, and try 
	dpkg -i *_amd64.deb
which should install jove, perhaps with a warning, then test jove
then uninstall with
	dpkg -r jove

Repeat for *_i386.deb

On a Linux machine or container, make a temp directory and
one at a time, unpack *-static.tar.gz in that directory, and
run jove-*-alpine-linux-musl-static/jove (i.e. twice for
i586 and x86_64)

Test the mingw tars on Windows VMs (I usually try a WinXP
and Win7 VM, and a real Win11 box).

Test the DOS self-extracting version in a new dosbox, and/or
a FreeDOS VM.

For minimal testing (need to improve this):

I usually ensure that the teachjove command works, that
describe-command works, run a shell command, test an
interactive shell, do a spell-check, unpack jove*.tgz and if
not Windows, run compile-it in that directory using
./jmake.sh as the compiler command, look up a tag, fill a
para, change right and left and try again.  delete a
close-brace and reinsert it to make sure formatting works,
same for a paren.  Hop around screen and file (forward and
back for chars, words, paras, screen, start and end of file;
then with repeats).  Turn on line numbering and hop around.
Turn off numbering and hop around.  Copy-paste with and
without xt-mouse.
